.TH QCCTL 8 "2026-10-25" "qcctl" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
qcctl \- apply power, clock and fan profiles on an Arduino Ventuno Q
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qcctl
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qcctl
sets CPU, GPU and thermal policy on an Arduino Ventuno Q (Qualcomm QCS8275 /
Dragonwing IQ\-8275) from named profiles in
.IR /etc/qcctl/profiles.conf .
It is the acting half of a pair:
.BR qcstats (1)
observes and
.B qcctl
acts. It contains no monitoring code and never reads a sensor directly \- a running
.BR qcctld (8)
owns the single sampler, and this command reads what the daemon publishes.
.PP
Applying a profile is
.BR idempotent :
settings the previous profile owned and the new one does not mention are reverted to
the saved boot state, so the result depends on which profile is applied and not on the
order profiles were applied in. What was reverted is reported rather than implied.
.PP
A profile is applied
.B whole or not at all.
If a write fails partway, the writes already made are rolled back: leaving the machine
in a state no configuration describes is worse than failing.
.PP
Most operations write to
.I /sys
and need root.
.BR \-\-query ,
.BR \-\-list
and
.B \-\-cool\-max
do not \-
.B \-\-cool\-max
asks the daemon over a socket that can only ever
.I raise
cooling.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-query
Report the current state and exit.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List the profiles in the configuration.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mode " " \fIname\fR|\fIid\fR
Apply a profile.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
With
.BR \-m :
print the writes and perform none.
.TP
.B \-\-reset
Restore the saved boot state.
.TP
.B \-\-save\-state
Capture the current state as the restore point.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Parse and validate the configuration, then plan every profile against the running
kernel, and exit. The second half is what catches a well\-formed file that names a
control this kernel does not expose; see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
.TP
.B \-\-reapply
Re\-apply the profile named in the active file. Used by the resume hook, and a no\-op
when no profile is in force.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-disruptive
Permit settings whose effect is drastic. Currently only offlining cpu0, the boot CPU.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output for
.B \-q
and
.BR \-l .
Consumers must use this and never the human layout.
.TP
.B \-\-fan\-baseline capture|show
Capture this board's healthy rpm\-per\-state reference, or print the stored one. A
capture drives the fan and takes about two minutes;
.B \-\-force
accepts values that look obstructed. The delta that judges against the reference lives
in the
.B < AIRFLOW >
block of
.IR /etc/qcctl/fan.config .
.TP
.B \-\-cool\-max
Run the fan at full speed until stopped with Ctrl\-C or
.BR SIGTERM .
Needs no privilege while
.BR qcctld (8)
is running, because it asks the daemon rather than driving the fan itself.
.TP
.BI \-c " path" "\fR, \fP" \-\-config " path"
Profiles file. Default
.IR /etc/qcctl/profiles.conf .
.TP
.BI \-\-state " path"
Boot\-state snapshot. Default
.IR /var/lib/qcctl/boot-state .
.TP
.BI \-\-active\-file " path"
Default
.IR /run/qcctl/active .
.TP
.BI \-\-selection\-file " path"
Where
.B \-m
records the operator's choice, so that a daemon restart honours it instead of
reverting to the DEFAULT profile. Scoped to the boot id: a power cycle returns to
DEFAULT. Default
.IR /var/lib/qcctl/selected .
.TP
.BI \-\-status\-file " path"
Where to read the daemon's published temperature, for
.BR \-\-cool\-max 's
handback decision. Default
.IR /run/qcctl/status .
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The operation was refused or could not be completed \- no daemon to ask for
.BR \-\-cool\-max ,
a write that failed and was rolled back.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, or a configuration file that does not parse. Fix the syntax at the
reported line.
.TP
.B 3
The file parses but is not a usable configuration: a bad setting, or no profile marked
DEFAULT. Fix what the message names.
.TP
.B 4
The file is
.BR fine ;
this kernel does not expose something it names. Not a configuration bug \- the board
changed under the file, typically after a kernel update.
.PP
2, 3 and 4 are kept distinct because they call for different fixes, and
.I install.sh
treats 4 separately so that an install after a kernel bump does not send anyone hunting
a syntax error that is not there.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/qcctl/profiles.conf
The profiles. Settings are user\-authored: unset is not the same as a default, and
units are mandatory.
.TP
.I /etc/qcctl/fan.config
Fan strategy \- one
.B < CURVE >
(open loop, a temperature\-to\-state lookup with hysteresis) or
.B < TARGET >
(closed loop, a temperature to hold) \- and the
.B < AIRFLOW >
block.
.TP
.I /etc/qcctl/*.dist
Reference copies of what this release ships, for comparison against yours.
.TP
.I /var/lib/qcctl/boot-state
What the machine looked like before anything was applied;
.B \-\-reset
restores this.
.TP
.I /var/lib/qcctl/fan-baseline
Measured rpm per fan state. Data, not configuration \- do not hand\-edit it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/qcctl/install.manifest
Every path the installer created, and what it did with each.
.TP
.I /run/qcctl/status
.TQ
.I /run/qcctl/active
.TQ
.I /run/qcctl/control
Published by the daemon; see
.BR qcctld (8).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QCCTL_SYSROOT
Read and write under this directory instead of
.IR / ,
so the tests can drive the whole program against a captured tree. Setting it also
relaxes the privilege check, because nothing it can reach is the real system.
.B Not for production use.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR qcctld (8),
.BR qcstats (1)
.PP
.I /usr/share/doc/qcctl/USAGE.md
documents every command in full, both configuration formats, and how to read the
status output.
.SH AUTHOR
Hans Kramer.
